WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.720
hi it's michelle from lab muffin beauty
science chemistry phd and skincare nerd

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:12.559
today we're talking about alcohol
not drinking alcohol alcohol in skincare

00:00:12.559 --> 00:00:18.800
i knew that
alcohol is one of the most controversial

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:22.400
ingredients in skincare
you'll hear things like alcohol can dry

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.960
out your skin give you premature
wrinkles

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.640
and kill your skin cells but is this
really true?

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:32.079
i've been looking at what the research
says about this topic with stephen

00:00:32.079 --> 00:00:35.280
hi everyone i'm stephen from Kind of
Stephen i'm

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.960
a cosmetic formulator and a friend of
michelle's and you can find me on

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.760
instagram
@kindofstephen today we're going to

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:44.079
be talking about the alcohols in
skincare

00:00:44.079 --> 00:00:47.600
why they're there and whether they're
worth avoiding in particular we're going

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:51.920
to be talking about ethyl alcohol or
ethanol or denatured alcohol

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:55.760
since this is the main controversial
alcohol if you like this sort of video

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.079
please give it a thumbs up subscribe to
my channel

00:00:58.079 --> 00:01:02.640
and click the notification bell let's
start by looking at exactly what alcohol

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:05.280
is
in chemistry an alcohol is any

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.799
carbon-based compound that contains an
oxygen bonded to a hydrogen

00:01:08.799 --> 00:01:14.799
an OH or hydroxyl group so for example
all of these chemicals are alcohols but

00:01:14.799 --> 00:01:18.159
just because they're alcohols doesn't
mean that they have the same properties

00:01:18.159 --> 00:01:20.960
on your skin
for example glycerin and glycols are

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.000
humectant moisturizers that will hydrate
your skin

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.040
sucrose is table sugar cetyl alcohol is
a fatty alcohol which doesn't evaporate

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:31.360
much at all and it feels oily on your
skin

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:35.119
for some people it's beneficial as a
moisturizer in other people it breaks

00:01:35.119 --> 00:01:38.960
them out
so yes some alcohols can be beneficial

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.799
for your skin but when people talk about
alcohols in skin care

00:01:42.799 --> 00:01:46.240
usually they're referring to the more
volatile alcohols which are the ones

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:50.159
that evaporate quickly off your skin
these have a reputation for being drying

00:01:50.159 --> 00:01:53.439
and irritating
and in particular what they mean is

00:01:53.439 --> 00:01:58.000
usually ethanol
or ethyl alcohol so what is ethanol

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.320
ethanol ethyl alcohol or denatured
alcohol is mostly the same alcohol that

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:06.799
you find in alcoholic drinks
so i think there's some confusion about

00:02:06.799 --> 00:02:10.319
what denatured alcohol is
i've read online that it's harsher for

00:02:10.319 --> 00:02:14.640
your skin that it's more gentle
that it's another molecule altogether

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:18.319
and that's not the case
so it's still in this case ethanol the

00:02:18.319 --> 00:02:20.080
same molecule
except there's been different

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.599
ingredients that have been added in to
discourage people from drinking it

00:02:23.599 --> 00:02:27.520
why is alcohol in skin care products
ethanol is included in skin care

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.840
products for a few different reasons
stephen is a cosmetic formulator who has

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.120
lots of experience in using different
ingredients in skin care products

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.720
so he's going to take us through some
of these roles so ethanol

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.640
is a really useful solvent it can
dissolve things that normally can

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:44.720
dissolve in water but it can also
dissolve

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.959
things that normally don't dissolve in
water so a lot of fragrances for example

00:02:48.959 --> 00:02:52.480
will come in
a ethanol or alcohol base and that's

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:56.959
because the fragrance oils and perfumes
that don't normally dissolve in water

00:02:56.959 --> 00:03:01.040
are able to dissolve in alcohol
salicylic acid for example is an

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.280
anti-acne ingredient so it has a very
low solubility in water but

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:09.680
actually dissolves quite readily in
ethanol so a lot of older formulations

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.840
with salicylic acid you'll usually see
an ethanol component so ethanol can also

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:17.599
be used as
a solvent to extract ingredients from

00:03:17.599 --> 00:03:21.040
plant material for example like vanilla
extract it can also be used to

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:24.319
remove lipids oils and waxes from the
skin

00:03:24.319 --> 00:03:27.440
if you look at older toner formulations
and

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:31.200
makeup removers a lot of them also had
ethanol in them to help remove

00:03:31.200 --> 00:03:34.959
makeup soil and dirt this is a lot less
common now

00:03:34.959 --> 00:03:39.599
but a high concentration of ethanol can
also be used as a preservative in a

00:03:39.599 --> 00:03:43.599
cosmetic formula so another property of
ethanol is that it's very volatile which

00:03:43.599 --> 00:03:47.200
means that it's very fast evaporating
so that gives it a very quick spreading

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.840
texture and also like a very quick
drying

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:54.400
cooling effect on the skin that's often
used in sunscreens to make their texture

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:00.000
lighter and help them evaporate and set
faster ethanol can also be used as a

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.519
skin penetration enhancer for certain
ingredients and that means it'll help

00:04:03.519 --> 00:04:07.439
get those ingredients deeper into the
skin at a higher concentration than

00:04:07.439 --> 00:04:11.040
without it
so a lot of formulas are now replacing

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:14.640
ethanol with
glycols like propanediol or propylene

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.040
glycol propanediol and propylene glycol
both

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:21.519
have alcohol functional groups on them
that OH group but they don't have the

00:04:21.519 --> 00:04:25.199
volatility
the fast evaporation that ethanol does

00:04:25.199 --> 00:04:29.040
so they actually remain on the skin
and they can give like that glowy

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:32.639
glistening effect on the skin
so that's good for some formulations but

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:37.199
not necessarily all formulations
i chatted to susan of mixed makeup about

00:04:37.199 --> 00:04:41.280
some products she uses that have alcohol
in them and what it's doing in there

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:44.400
i've put the link to her video in the
description so

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:49.360
alcohol is pretty useful for beauty
products but does it have a dark side?

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:53.280
so i generally try to avoid making
formulations with ethanol if it can be

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:55.280
avoided
that's not necessarily because i think

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.600
it's dangerous or harmful just that i
know

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.520
that people in the beauty community
really aren't looking for formulations

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:05.680
with ethanol anymore
there are lots of in vitro studies where

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:08.880
the effects of alcohol on cells in a
dish were studied

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.280
and the results of these are pretty
scary alcohol can make cells die in a

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:16.880
process called apoptosis
it can make cells produce inflammatory

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:21.680
signals it can denature proteins
and it can stop enzymes from working and

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:24.080
we know that alcohol is a really good
disinfectant

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:27.840
it can kill bacteria very quickly but
skin is very different from naked cells

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:30.639
in a dish
or a single bacterium made up of just

00:05:30.639 --> 00:05:34.880
one cell we have about 10 to 20 layers
of dead skin cells on the surface of our

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:37.840
skin
the stratum corneum before we get to the

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:41.120
living layers of cells
and these dead cells aren't just any

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.840
dead cells they're toughened with
keratin

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:47.520
and they're sitting in an oily goop
which is really really good at

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:49.840
preventing things from entering and
leaving

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:53.840
so for ethanol to affect our living skin
cells like it does in the in vitro

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:56.000
studies
it would firstly have to get through

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:59.840
this extremely efficient barrier
in a high enough concentration to make a

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.520
difference while most of these in vitro
studies used relatively

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:07.120
low concentrations of ethanol somewhere
around 0.5 percent

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:11.600
they also left the cells in contact with
the ethanol for a really long time

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:16.319
sometimes 24 hours in a container where
the alcohol couldn't evaporate

00:06:16.319 --> 00:06:20.080
but ethanol when we apply it to our skin
is very volatile

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:24.160
it evaporates very easily and you'll
have felt this when you feel the cool

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:26.560
sensation from an alcohol-containing
product

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:30.560
this is the alcohol evaporating and
taking some of your body heat with it it

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:33.199
turns out that when you apply ethanol on
your skin

00:06:33.199 --> 00:06:38.000
the vast majority of it will evaporate
so one experiment used pig skin to take

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.720
a look at how quickly 100%
ethanol evaporated off the skin and what

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:46.319
they found was that after 10 seconds
more than 50% of it had already

00:06:46.319 --> 00:06:50.240
evaporated overall they found that
about three percent less than three

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:53.919
percent was left on the skin
now there's some issues with that three

00:06:53.919 --> 00:06:57.840
percent because they think that some
of the alcohol had actually leaked under

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:00.479
the chamber
of their test equipment so it's actually

00:07:00.479 --> 00:07:04.400
likely that it's much much less
so what we can infer from this is that

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:07.840
if you're using a skincare product with
ethanol in the formula

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:11.680
maybe around five to ten percent then
more than 97% of that is going to

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:15.199
evaporate off the skin
very quickly so all of these studies

00:07:15.199 --> 00:07:18.000
showing that alcohol was bad for cells
in a dish

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:22.319
we can't immediately apply them to the
way that we use alcohol in skin care the

00:07:22.319 --> 00:07:25.599
most relevant studies for us would be
the ones where they looked at alcohol

00:07:25.599 --> 00:07:29.280
being applied to human skin
and observing what happened as i've

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:32.800
mentioned before it's often really hard
to find good skin care studies because

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.160
most scientific research is funded by
the government so there needs to be a

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:38.880
really good public health reason for
doing the research

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:42.319
otherwise it might be funded by a beauty
brand or an ingredient company

00:07:42.319 --> 00:07:46.000
in which case there needs to be a strong
commercial reason for doing the study

00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:50.080
luckily there is a relevant situation
where people do end up applying lots and

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:53.919
lots of alcohol to their skin
in much larger quantities and much more

00:07:53.919 --> 00:07:57.840
often than we would in skincare that's
in healthcare where alcohol-based hand

00:07:57.840 --> 00:08:02.080
sanitizers are used by healthcare
workers to prevent spreading of diseases

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:05.440
in particular nurses have to disinfect
their hands between patients

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:08.560
and this adds up one study found that on
average

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:12.879
nurses had to clean their hands 55 times
a day lots of nurses end up with cracked

00:08:12.879 --> 00:08:16.639
skin irritation and blisters on their
hands from this repeated cleaning

00:08:16.639 --> 00:08:20.240
it's been found that skin irritation
makes people less likely to follow hand

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:23.039
hygiene procedures
and so there's a really compelling

00:08:23.039 --> 00:08:27.199
public health reason to research this
the main methods for disinfecting hands

00:08:27.199 --> 00:08:29.440
are washing your hands with soap and
water

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:32.880
and alcohol-based hand rubs so there's
been quite a few clinical studies

00:08:32.880 --> 00:08:35.519
comparing soap and water versus alcohol
hand rubs

00:08:35.519 --> 00:08:39.360
and between different hand rub formulas
on things like skin irritation

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:43.919
dryness cracking and so on so what do
these studies tell us

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:47.120
what a lot of these studies are showing
us is that even using

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:51.519
a very large amount of ethanol in the
skin doesn't really have a huge

00:08:51.519 --> 00:08:53.080
long-term impact

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:54.540
A-WHAAAAAAA

00:08:54.540 --> 00:08:56.880
let's look at one of
the more recent detailed studies on

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:01.200
alcohol-based hand rubs to get an idea
of how these studies are conducted this

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:05.200
article which i've linked in the
description was published in 2017

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:09.200
in the international journal of cosmetic
science it's open access so you can

00:09:09.200 --> 00:09:12.480
download it and read it as well the
clinical part of this study involved

00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:15.920
testing three different alcohols at 70
concentration

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:19.600
on the inner forearms of 25 white female
volunteers

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:24.720
aged 35 to 50. a fourth area of skin had
water applied as a comparison the

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:27.519
solutions were rubbed into skin for
about 10 seconds

00:09:27.519 --> 00:09:32.000
either 20 times a day or 100 times a day
the skin was also washed six times a day

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:35.680
with hand wash and water to mimic what
would happen for a typical nurse

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:39.839
this process was done for 14 days around
every two days the scientists measured

00:09:39.839 --> 00:09:43.040
skin hydration
redness and transepidermal water loss

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:46.720
this is how much water was evaporating
through the skin a way of measuring skin

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:49.760
barrier function
so these hand rub studies usually use

00:09:49.760 --> 00:09:53.600
alcohol concentrations far higher than
we would see in skincare plus they used

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:57.519
it many more times a day plus there's
that extra irritation from the repeated

00:09:57.519 --> 00:10:01.200
cleansing as well it's likely that
whatever effects these studies find will

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:04.000
be a bigger version of what would happen
to us

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:08.480
when we apply a moisturizer or sunscreen
with 5 or 10% ethanol in them

00:10:08.480 --> 00:10:12.640
so what were the findings of these
studies one of the biggest questions is

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:16.079
does alcohol cause
inflammation? inflammation isn't good for

00:10:16.079 --> 00:10:20.480
skin it's been linked to lots of skin
issues like acne rosacea and premature

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:22.959
aging
and in some in vitro cell studies

00:10:22.959 --> 00:10:27.040
alcohol does seem to be able to cause
cells to release inflammatory markers

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:30.800
but will this happen on actual skin? in
these hand rub studies

00:10:30.800 --> 00:10:34.880
inflammation was measured by looking at
skin redness inflammation causes blood

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:38.399
flow to increase
and so you get a reddening effect and in

00:10:38.399 --> 00:10:41.440
several of these studies
scientists found that ethanol didn't

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:45.120
seem to be linked to skin redness
compared to no ethanol this was even the

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:48.480
case in a study where 70% ethanol was
applied to the skin

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:52.800
a hundred times a day for 14 days there
was no statistically significant

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:56.399
difference in redness compared to water
applied 100 times a day

00:10:56.399 --> 00:11:01.519
so that is a total of 1400 applications
of ethanol over two weeks

00:11:01.519 --> 00:11:05.360
another question about alcohol is
whether or not it can disrupt your skin

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:09.279
barrier to a harmful extent
your skin's main job is to act as a

00:11:09.279 --> 00:11:12.240
barrier between you
and the outside world the top dead

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:16.160
layers of skin the stratum corneum
are essential to this function to act as

00:11:16.160 --> 00:11:19.920
an effective barrier this stratum
corneum has to keep itself maintained

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:22.399
the dead skin cells have to continuously
shed

00:11:22.399 --> 00:11:26.399
smoothly and the oily lipids in between
the cells have to stay in an organized

00:11:26.399 --> 00:11:29.360
formation
on isolated skin samples in vitro

00:11:29.360 --> 00:11:32.720
enzymes that are important in cell
shedding and in the production of oily

00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:35.839
lipids can be disturbed
and so they don't work as well there are

00:11:35.839 --> 00:11:38.560
also some in vitro studies that suggest
that alcohol

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:43.200
can temporarily modify how molecules are
arranged in your skin or it can remove

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:46.240
lipids from your skin
in a process called lipid extraction

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:48.880
this isn't like it's making your skin
fall apart or anything

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:52.320
it's much more subtle microscopic
changes for example

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:55.360
how the oily molecules are arranged
relative to each other

00:11:55.360 --> 00:11:58.560
and this effect doesn't last for long
it's thought that this is how

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:02.320
ethanol helps ingredients penetrate your
skin but these findings aren't really

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:05.120
consistent
many times skin samples even if they

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:07.440
were left with alcohol for a really long
time

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:11.360
there weren't big negative effects and
many of these studies do actually point

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:14.480
out that the conditions that they use
don't reflect how we would use

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:18.560
alcohol on our skin in the clinical
studies ethanol doesn't really seem to

00:12:18.560 --> 00:12:21.600
disrupt the barrier much
skin barrier disruption is usually

00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:25.279
measured through transepidermal water
loss this is how much water is escaping

00:12:25.279 --> 00:12:28.160
through skin
into the air and so a more disrupted

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:31.519
barrier would cause
greater water loss in multiple hand rub

00:12:31.519 --> 00:12:34.720
studies changes in transepidermal water
loss were minor

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:38.720
even with 20 applications of alcohol a
day there was a measurable difference in

00:12:38.720 --> 00:12:42.240
one study but that was when ethanol was
applied a hundred times a day

00:12:42.240 --> 00:12:46.079
much more than we would in skincare
unless you have a very strange skincare

00:12:46.079 --> 00:12:48.480
routine
one thing that can seem to happen with

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:52.720
alcohol is dehydration there have been
mixed results in clinical studies

00:12:52.720 --> 00:12:56.320
some have found that alcohol does
decrease hydration while others haven't

00:12:56.320 --> 00:13:00.160
for example in the Cartner study that i
mentioned earlier skin hydration wasn't

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:03.760
significantly different between ethanol
and water whether you applied it 20

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:08.000
times a day or 100 times a day
in another study skin oil content didn't

00:13:08.000 --> 00:13:11.600
change significantly
after applying hand rubs containing 60

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:14.880
to 80%
alcohol 50 times a day for a week

00:13:14.880 --> 00:13:18.399
however there are ways to help with
dehydration and dryness

00:13:18.399 --> 00:13:22.240
one study looked at adding moisturizing
ingredients like glycerin and lanolin

00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:26.480
and fatty alcohols to hand rubs this
study used hand rubs containing propanol

00:13:26.480 --> 00:13:30.000
which has been found in other studies to
be more irritating than ethanol they

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:32.639
found that these moisturizing
ingredients decreased the amount of

00:13:32.639 --> 00:13:35.040
redness and dryness that the propanol
caused

00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:39.120
in skin care propanol is really rare and
we almost always find ethanol combined

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:43.199
with these moisturizing ingredients
what about stinging? one reason that we

00:13:43.199 --> 00:13:46.720
tend to be a bit scared of alcohol is
that you sometimes get that painful

00:13:46.720 --> 00:13:49.600
stinging sensation
but multiple studies have found that

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:53.360
that stinging painful sensation
doesn't actually mean that harm is being

00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:56.560
done to your skin
alcohol does tend to sting your skin if

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:59.519
it's already damaged
the alcohol can go through the skin and

00:13:59.519 --> 00:14:02.959
touch the nerve endings
which caused the pain this is like a

00:14:02.959 --> 00:14:06.399
more sensitive version of what happens
when water gets into a paper cut

00:14:06.399 --> 00:14:10.320
so what does this mean for alcohol in
skin care these studies on alcohol hand

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:13.120
rubs aren't done under the same
conditions as we would use

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:17.440
alcohol in skincare but they're a lot
closer than in vitro studies the studies

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:19.839
were mostly on
arm skin which is thicker and less

00:14:19.839 --> 00:14:23.839
sensitive than face skin plus these
studies only lasted a week or two but

00:14:23.839 --> 00:14:26.399
